"LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15515)\nciteturn3396academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Title: LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference ... Date: Fri Sep 19 01:39:08 2025\nTitle: LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference\nAuthors: Hantao Yang, Hong Xie, Defu Lian, Enhong Chen\nDate: Fri Sep 19 01:39:08 2025\n\nThis paper revisits the LLM cache bandit problem, with a special focus on addressing the query heterogeneity for cost-effective LLM inference. Previous works often assume uniform query sizes. Heterogeneous query sizes introduce a combinatorial structure for cache selection, making the cache replacement process more computationally and statistically challenging. We treat optimal cache selection as a knapsack problem and employ an accumulation-based strategy to effectively balance computational overhead and cache updates. In theoretical analysis, we prove that the regret of our algorithm achieves an $O(\\sqrt{MNT})$ bound, improving the coefficient of $\\sqrt{MN}$ compared to the $O(MN\\sqrt{T})$ result in Berkeley, where $N$ is the total number of queries and $M$ is the cache size. Additionally, we also provide a problem-dependent bound, which was absent in previous works. The experiment rely on real-world data show that our algorithm reduces the total cost by approximately 12\\%.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRepository Planning Graph (RPG) (https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/repository-planning-graph-rpg)\nciteturn3396search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 3 months ago; Updated 23 September 2025 ... RPG formalizes repository construction as a graph planning task, confronting a core bottleneck: LLMs excel at generating functions or files, but struggle to generate entire repositories due to the scale, complexity, and dependency structure involved (Luo et al., 19 Sep 2025).\n\n# Repository Planning Graph (RPG)\n\nUpdated 23 September 2025\n\n  * Repository Planning Graph (RPG) is a unified, graph-based representation that encodes repository functionalities and file structures into a coherent blueprint.\n  * It enables scalable, interpretable codebase generation by mapping high-level capabilities to detailed implementation artifacts through layered graph semantics.\n  * It supports debugging, navigation, and targeted revisions by using explicit ordering and data-flow edges to manage complex dependencies.\n\nA Repository Planning Graph (RPG) is a persistent, unified graph-based representation designed for scalable and interpretable codebase generation. It encodes functional and implementation-level planning—including capabilities, file structures, data-flow, and specific functions—within a connected blueprint, replacing the ambiguity of natural language with explicit structure. RPG provides a substrate on which software agents and LLMs can realize coherent, long-horizon planning and synthesis of software repositories, enabling stepwise refinement from broad specification to executable artifacts (Luo et al., 19 Sep 2025).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/audio/2509.16198v1)\nciteturn3396search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 2 months ago; # RPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation ... September 19, 2025\n\n# RPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation\n\n# RPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation\n\nSeptember 19, 2025\n\nImage: Tsinghua University logoTsinghua UniversityImage: University of California, San Diego logoUniversity of California, San Diego\n\nJane Luo, Xin Zhang, Steven Liu, Jie Wu, Yiming Huang, Yangyu Huang, Chengyu Yin, Ying Xin, Jianfeng Liu, Yuefeng Zhan, Hao Sun, Qi Chen, Scarlett Li, Mao Yang\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation (https://airaxiv.com/arxiv/pdf/3056/)\nciteturn3396search13 [wordlim: 200] A PREPRINT - SEPTEMBER 22, 2025 ... Further analysis shows that the Repository Planning Graph (RPG) captures complex dependencies, including inter-module data flows and class-level relations.\nA PREPRINT - SEPTEMBER 22, 2025\n\nframeworks [11–14], where intermediate plans are externalized (e.g., in markdown files) and refined across iterations to connect intent with execution.\n\nWhile these paradigms differ in execution, they share a critical vulnerability: reliance on natural language as the intermediate representation for planning. We argue this is a fundamental limitation. Natural language, while ideal for human-computer interaction, is inherently ambiguous (making intent/constraints hard to disambiguate in code search [15]), unstructured (lacking explicit hierarchy and dependency tracking for complex reasoning [16]), and fragile over long horizons (static plans degrade across iterations without adaptive adjustment [17]). When extended to automatic repository generation, this limitation yields two failure modes: (1) unstable proposal-level planning, where generated functionalities are incomplete, overlapping, or unevenly scoped, making it difficult to ensure systematic coverage of requirements [18]; and (2) fragmented implementation-level planning, where function implementations and architectural plans drift across iterations, causing inconsistencies in dependencies, data flows, and modular boundaries [19, 20].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference (https://www.catalyzex.com/paper/llm-cache-bandit-revisited-addressing-query)\nciteturn3396search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Title:LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference ... Sep 19, 2025\n\n# Title:LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference\n\n### Paper and Code\n\nRequest Code\n\n\nSep 19, 2025\n\nAuthors:Hantao Yang, Hong Xie, Defu Lian, Enhong Chen\n\nFigures and Tables:\n\nAbstract:This paper revisits the LLM cache bandit problem, with a special focus on addressing the query heterogeneity for cost-effective LLM inference. Previous works often assume uniform query sizes. Heterogeneous query sizes introduce a combinatorial structure for cache selection, making the cache replacement process more computationally and statistically challenging. We treat optimal cache selection as a knapsack problem and employ an accumulation-based strategy to effectively balance computational overhead and cache updates. In theoretical analysis, we prove that the regret of our algorithm achieves an $O(\\sqrt{MNT})$ bound, improving the coefficient of $\\sqrt{MN}$ compared to the $O(MN\\sqrt{T})$ result in Berkeley, where $N$ is the total number of queries and $M$ is the cache size. Additionally, we also provide a problem-dependent bound, which was absent in previous works. The experiment rely on real-world data show that our algorithm reduces the total cost by approximately 12\\%.\n\nFull-text Links:View paper on--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZeroRepo: Graph-Guided Repo Generation (https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/zerorepo-framework)\nciteturn3396search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 2 months ago; Updated 25 September 2025 ... ZeroRepo is a graph-driven framework for generating large-scale, coherent software repositories from scratch, leveraging LLMs and a persistent graph representation known as the Repository Planning Graph (RPG).\n\n# ZeroRepo: Graph-Guided Repo Generation\n\nUpdated 25 September 2025\n\n  * ZeroRepo is a graph-driven framework that unifies high-level planning and low-level implementation via a persistent Repository Planning Graph (RPG).\n  * It orchestrates proposal-level planning, implementation refinement, and graph-guided code synthesis in a sequential, test-driven workflow.\n  * Empirical results on the RepoCraft benchmark highlight its scalability by generating repositories over 36,000 LOC with high functional coverage and improved test pass rates.\n\nZeroRepo is a graph-driven framework for generating large-scale, coherent software repositories from scratch, leveraging LLMs and a persistent graph representation known as the Repository Planning Graph (RPG). By explicitly modeling both the high-level functionality and low-level structural dependencies of repositories, ZeroRepo enables LLM-based agents to conduct unified, scalable planning and test-driven code generation, systematically advancing the state of automated end-to-end software synthesis.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRepository Planning Graph for Code Generation | PDF | Machine Learning | Modular Programming (https://www.scribd.com/document/920560878/A-Repository-Planning-Graph-for-Unified-and-Scalable-Codebase-Generation)\nciteturn3396search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last year; file-augmented graph and (B2) data-flow/function encoding into the final Repository Planning Graph (RPG). ... ## 3 Repository Planning Graph Construction ... A PREPRINT - S EPTEMBER 22, 2025\nsrc/algos\nML\nAlgorithms Regression\n\nnatural language descriptions. Its nodes carry dual seman- class BaseEstimator\nElastic class\nTraining Data / Predictions ElasticNet(BaseEstimator)\ntics. At the functional level, they represent progressively Feature Matrix\nNet\n\nrefined capabilities: high-level modules (e.