e *:\n\\ begin { verbatim }\ns e a r c h _ i n t e r f a c e _ b y _ f u n c t i o n a l i t y ([ ’ optimize ’ , ’ initialize ’])\n\\ end { verbatim }\n\n## ### Termination Tool\n\n- ‘ Terminate ( result ) ‘\nTerminates the localization exploration and returns the final ranked list of located interfaces .\nThe result must follow the specified JSON - style format , including file path and interface\ntype ( function , class , or method ) .\n* Usage *: Invoked after completing exploration to deliver the final interface localization results\n.\n* Example *:\n\\ begin { verbatim }\nTerminate ( result =[\n{\" file_path \": \" t o p 1 _ f i l e _ f u l l p a t h . py \" , \" interface \": \" method : Class1 . function1 \"} ,\n{\" file_path \": \" t o p 2 _ f i l e _ f u l l p a t h . py \" , \" interface \": \" function : function2 \"} ,\n{\" file_path \": \" t o p 3 _ f i l e _ f u l l p a t h . py \" , \" interface \": \" class : Class3 \"} ,\n])\n\\ end { verbatim }\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLLM inference Arxiv Daily | Automatically Update inference Papers Daily using Github Actions (Update Every 8th hours) (https://toseic.github.io/LLM-inference-arxiv-daily/)\nciteturn3396search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; 2025-09-19  | LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference  | Hantao Yang et.al.\n2025-09-22  | Cronus: Efficient LLM inference on Heterogeneous GPU Clusters via Partially Disaggregated Prefill  | Yunzhao Liu et.al.  | 2509.17357  | null\n2025-09-22  | Multi-View Attention Multiple-Instance Learning Enhanced by LLM Reasoning for Cognitive Distortion Detection  | Jun Seo Kim et.al.  | 2509.17292  | null\n2025-09-21  | MoA-Off: Adaptive Heterogeneous Modality-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference  | Zheming Yang et.al.  | 2509.16995  | null\n2025-09-20  | Shift Parallelism: Low-Latency, High-Throughput LLM Inference for Dynamic Workloads  | Mert Hidayetoglu et.al.  | 2509.16495  | null\n2025-09-19  | LightCode: Compiling LLM Inference for Photonic-Electronic Systems  | Ryan Tomich et.al.  | 2509.16443  | null\n2025-09-19  | LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference  | Hantao Yang et.al.  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRepository Planning Graphs: How Microsoft’s ICLR 2026 Architecture Beats Claude Code on Whole-Codebase Generation — and What It Means for Codex CLI | Codex Knowledge Base (https://codex.danielvaughan.com/2026/09/03/rpg-repository-planning-graph-whole-codebase-generation-codex-cli/)\nciteturn3396search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; September 3, 2026 ... A new ICLR 2026 paper from Microsoft Research proposes a fix: replace natural language as the planning medium with a first-class graph structure called a Repository Planning Graph (RPG). ... Proposal-level planning transforms the specification into a functionality graph by sampling from the EpiCoder Feature Tree — a pre-built taxonomy of 1.5 million software capabilities.\n\nRepository Planning Graphs: How Microsoft's ICLR 2026 Architecture Beats Claude Code on Whole-Codebase Generation — and What It Means for Codex CLI\n\nSeptember 3, 2026\n\nUpdated October 6, 2026\n\n# Repository Planning Graphs: How Microsoft’s ICLR 2026 Architecture Beats Claude Code on Whole-Codebase Generation — and What It Means for Codex CLI\n\n* * *\n\nThe dominant failure mode of today’s coding agents is not line-by-line quality — it is structural coherence at scale. Ask any current agent to generate a complete repository and you will get code that compiles and even passes narrow tests, but whose module boundaries are arbitrary, whose data flows are ad hoc, and whose file organisation bears no relationship to the feature hierarchy in the original specification. A new ICLR 2026 paper from Microsoft Research proposes a fix: replace natural language as the planning medium with a first-class graph structure called a Repository Planning Graph (RPG).^{1} The companion framework, ZeroRepo, outperforms Claude Code on the RepoCraft benchmark by 27.3 percentage points on functionality coverage and 35.8 points on test pass rate.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) RPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395710001_RPG_A_Repository_Planning_Graph_for_Unified_and_Scalable_Codebase_Generation)\nciteturn3396search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; A PREPRINT - S E PT EM BER 22, 2025 ... ﬁle-augmented graph and (B2) data-ﬂow/function encoding into the ﬁnal Repository Planning Graph (RPG).\n\narchitectural skeletons and then expand into detailed code. Externalized iterative planning adopts general-purpose agent\n\n∗Equal contribution\n\n†Corresponding author: xinzhang3@microsoft.com\n\narXiv:2509.16198v1 [cs.CL] 19 Sep 2025\n\nA PREPRINT - S E PT EM BER 22, 2025\n\nframeworks [\n\n11\n\n–\n\n14\n\n], where intermediate plans are externalized (e.g., in markdown ﬁles) and reﬁned across iterations to\n\nconnect intent with execution.\n\nWhile these paradigms differ in execution, they share a critical vulnerability: reliance on natural language as the\n\nintermediate representation for planning. We argue this is a fundamental limitation. Natural language, while ideal\n\nfor human-computer interaction, is inherently ambiguous (making intent/constraints hard to disambiguate in code\n\nsearch [\n\n15\n\n]), unstructured (lacking explicit hierarchy and dependency tracking for complex reasoning [\n\n16\n\n]), and fragile\n\nover long horizons (static plans degrade across iterations without adaptive adjustment [\n\n17\n\n]). When extended to automatic\n\nrepository generation, this limitation yields two failure modes: (1) unstable proposal-level planning, where generated\n\nfunctionalities are incomplete, overlapping, or unevenly scoped, making it difﬁcult to ensure systematic coverage of\n\nrequirements [\n\n18\n\n]; and (2) fragmented implementation-level planning, where function implementations and architectural\n\nplans drift across iterations, causing inconsistencies in dependencies, data ﬂows, and modular boundaries [19, 20].\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference. (https://dblp1.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2509-15515.html)\nciteturn3396search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # \"LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for ...\" ... (2025) ... LLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference.\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2509-15515\n\nHantao Yang, Hong Xie, Defu Lian, Enhong Chen:\nLLM Cache Bandit Revisited: Addressing Query Heterogeneity for Cost-Effective LLM Inference. CoRR abs/2509.15515 (2025)\n\nImage\n\n[Input]load links from unpaywall.org\n\nPrivacy notice: By enabling the option above, your browser will contact the API of unpaywall.org to load hyperlinks to open access articles. Although we do not have any reason to believe that your call will be tracked, we do not have any control over how the remote server uses your data. So please proceed with care and consider checking the Unpaywall privacy policy.\n\nArchived links via Wayback Machine\n\nFor web page which are no longer available, try to retrieve content from the of the Internet Archive (if available).\n\nOpenAlex data\n\nLoad additional information about publications from .\n\n[Input]load data from openalex.org\n\nPrivacy notice: By enabling the option above, your browser will contact the AP